/** Editorial translations — siman 124 / turei-zahav (21 blocks) */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  '2#_': `And there are those who are lenient even in touch of a ger toshav. This is the Rosh who wrote that even though regarding a purchased slave from idolaters who circumcised and did not immerse he makes stam yeinam, as appears in seif 3 — there it is different since his heart is not for Heaven but he becomes Jewish from fear of his master; and in the Tur this distinction is explained according to Rabbenu Tam who rules regarding a slave who circumcised and immersed that we still require twelve months — he wrote on this: and specifically when he does not convert willingly; but if he converted willingly immediately after he circumcised and immersed, etc.; and he does not want to distinguish regarding the slave himself whether he converts willingly or against his will, for all willingly is also against his will since his heart is not whole in the matter; and likewise explicitly in Tosafot perek R' Yishmael (Avodah Zarah daf 57) that you do not circumcise a slave against his will except nevertheless fear of his master is upon him; and it appears a word is missing in the Tur and it must read: and specifically a slave who does not convert, etc.; and such is the text in Mordechai — the essence of the distinction is between slave and other convert; but regarding the slave itself there is no distinction at all here; and if he converts explicitly against his will it does not help at all, as we establish siman 267; and Rashal wrote that Karaites have the law of a ger toshav and it is permitted to drink with them wine of Israel.`,
  '4#_': `Since they circumcised, etc. He follows his reason, for he wrote in Beit Yosef that Rambam also holds like Ra'ah that we require specifically that they circumcised, unlike Rashba who holds even if they did not circumcise; and I am very astonished whence Beit Yosef infers this from Rambam's words, for behold in the Gemara it also states circumcised and Rashba explained the circumcised stated is to include adults — even if circumcised they forbid; and why should we not say likewise in Rambam; and further it is difficult why he did not rule like Rashba, since we explicitly say so in Tosefta as brought in Terumat HaDeshen; and on the Tur it is difficult that he did not see Sefer Terumat HaDeshen and did not know this Tosefta — certainly one has no power to disagree with Tosefta.`,
  '6#_': `Who is not an idolater, etc. Such as Ishmaelites; and he learns it from ger toshav — for what he ruled in seif 2 that he forbids only for drinking; and even though there he wrote he accepted upon himself seven commandments — here he informs us as a stringency that even so forbidden for drinking; but regarding prohibition of benefit we do not care, only regarding idolaters as already explained — prohibition of drinking is because of their daughters and prohibition of benefit is because of avodah zarah; therefore whoever is not an idolater there is no prohibition of benefit here; and according to "there are those who are lenient" that Rama brought there — nevertheless here we descend a level and at minimum permitted in benefit; and in the Tur he wrote in the name of Rashba that one should be lenient regarding Ishmaelites since they do not worship avodah zarah; and Derishah challenged from what Beit Yosef wrote above in this siman in the name of Rashba in this language — behold whoever accepted not to worship avodah zarah and seven Noahide commandments — their wine is not yayin nesech to forbid in benefit; and here it deals because he will not worship avodah zarah alone; and he expanded to resolve — and there is no difficulty at all, for there it means whether according to R' Meir if he accepted not to worship avodah zarah or according to the Sages if he accepted seven commandments — and we establish like the Sages who require accepting not to worship avodah zarah also seven commandments, and accordingly in this at least permitted; except some Geonim say one should be lenient even with not worshiping avodah zarah alone even for the Sages, from Rav Yehuda who sent to Avodarni on their festival day as Beit Yosef wrote; and there is astonishment in Beit Yosef's words who wrote to resolve Rambam's words to himself — R' Yehuda deals where the entire nation does not worship avodah zarah and the baraita deals where his nation worships avodah zarah; and it is difficult what the Gemara challenges "but it is taught: what is a ger toshav," etc. — namely R' Meir's words who requires acceptance before three; and it answers this is for sustaining him — and what difficulty at all, for Rav Yehuda deals where the entire nation does not worship avodah zarah, and if so the baraita also holds that when one accepts not to worship avodah zarah and the entire nation is so — R' Meir agrees that suffices; but if the nation worships avodah zarah — R' Meir and the rabbis disagree; and it is difficult to resolve.`,
  '11#ג': `A vessel that has spigots, etc. Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Rashba's responsum: the making of this vessel is called in the Gemara kani shekiz and I did not know what it is; nevertheless what is in its mouth is forbidden and all the wine is drawn after it — why should it be less than stream connection, etc. — end; and in Mishmeret HaBayit daf 134 Rashba also wrote on this that the form of kani shekiz was not clarified for us and one cannot permit on it through stream; and in any case it is unlike Rashi's explanation; and at the end he wrote that kani shekiz was in a manner where there was no stream connection; and Ran wrote in the name of Raavad to resolve that we do not say stream connection except when everything came out through that opening — which is not so here, for it comes out through the other hole where the non-Jew drinks; and in my humble opinion to resolve: all as long as he does not stir there is no prohibition, and all the time he drinks he does not stir but draws to himself; only when he stops and the wine slips from his mouth he stirs below — therefore there is no prohibition as long as the non-Jew did not stop; and in that vessel wine comes by itself into the reeds not through sucking — for if through sucking it would be the law of a spigot that I mentioned in siman 124 seif 24 in the name of Maharil; and for practical halachah it appears since this was not clarified for Rashba and he wrote that Raavad also was challenged on this — we have no leniency to learn from this vessel, and one should not drink with the non-Jew in any vessel.`,
  '12#א': `A non-Jew who entered, etc. This is Rambam's wording, only the author added the word "and he shook it" following the approach Kesef Mishnah wrote challenging Raavad: and this incident of a non-Jew who entered a shop and said "is there wine to buy," etc., and he put his hand on the leather bottle and shook it and said "this is not wine" and they forbade it in benefit because of the stirring — end; and Kesef Mishnah explained the essence of the challenge is that Rambam did not write "and he shook it" but simply "and touched," while in the Gemara it says "and he shook it"; and Darkhei Moshe resolved on this that Rambam did not need to write "and he shook it" since he already wrote in that chapter that touch alone does not forbid except stirring — end; and it is astonishing, for if so he should have brought only in this language the incident of a non-Jew who stirred; and further Raavad already wrote above in that chapter that we require stirring; and it appears Raavad comes here on two matters: first to challenge what Rambam wrote "he extended his hand while searching and touched wine" — he forbids it, for he intended for wine; and there is a difficulty: how do we know that at the time of that touch he intended touch — perhaps the touch itself was unintentional as a person searches; and if you say he intended touch — perhaps he thought it was water — end; he brings the Gemara that said "this is not wine" — through this it is known he intended touch in wine. Further Raavad comes here as proof for what he challenged at the beginning of the chapter regarding what Rambam wrote how is touch when he touched wine itself and stirred; but if he extended his hand to a barrel and they seized his hand before he removed it and did not shake it, etc. — the wine is not forbidden — it implies not forbidden at all; perforce all who did not stir but mere touch is permitted even for drinking; but if he stirred forbidden in benefit — on this Raavad disagrees above and said: when they seized his hand did he not touch wine? Rather they said thus: they seized his hand so he would not stir — for it does not forbid in benefit unless he stirs — end; perforce Raavad has prohibition of drinking even in mere touch without stirring; and Kesef Mishnah explains thus to resolve both Rambam's words; and Rashi also explains thus in perek R' Yishmael (Avodah Zarah daf 59); and perforce Raavad comes here to bring proof for this, that there is prohibition of drinking even without stirring — for behold in this incident where he was stirring they said of it forbidden in benefit because of stirring — it implies without stirring there is at least prohibition of drinking; and this is proven there in that discussion where they said stirring makes yayin nesech — it implies stirring causes yayin nesech which is prohibition of benefit; without stirring there is prohibition of drinking. And the Tur wrote in this law: and if it mixed with other wine the value of what was stirred is forbidden in benefit and the rest permitted in benefit; and it is difficult — obviously; who is worse than any stam yeinam that mixed, as below siman 134; granted in the Gemara it is difficult for us; but on the Tur it is difficult why need this; and one may say: since he stirred it is definite stam yeinam and has no remedy — it informs us it was only stam yeinam. Further there is extra language that he wrote "and he shook it in his hand" — this "in his hand" why? One may say it informs in this that he hinted with his hand — meaning "this is not wine" in wonder.`,
  '13#_': `And he will not shake it. Meaning: if he had stirred it would be forbidden after he took what fell there and wants to remove it; but without stirring it is not forbidden in benefit from mere touch alone; and that which he did not forbid the wine through inserting his hand — Tosafot and the Rosh explained: then he was occupied saving what fell to him and it is like measuring in seif 19; but in removal he is no longer occupied; and Levush compared to measuring that we mentioned, that there occupied even in removal since he must remove in a manner that he intends the measurement — unlike here where at the time of this removal he was without any occupation — end; therefore then guarding is needed lest he stir; and Tosafot wrote in "and what he wrote regarding stirring," etc., that from Tosafot's and the Rosh's words it appears if he intended to touch wine it would be forbidden even though he did not stir; and in my humble opinion it does not appear so, for it is obvious they also hold without stirring there is no prohibition of benefit except they needed to permit insertion because he was occupied; here where he wants to take from there what fell — impossible without stirring, for at the time of insertion he searches here and there — it fell; perforce it would be forbidden if not for occupation; and likewise proven from Terumat HaDeshen daf 134 who also gave occupation as reason; however he holds there that all touch through something else is without stirring unless he took it in his hand; and likewise proven from the Tur's words who ruled we require stirring and did not bring his father's view to disagree on this except as I explained; nevertheless for practical halachah we must be concerned for Rashba's words and forbid through touch alone, for impossible without stirring; and all the more for what Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Ran that touch alone also forbids.`,
  '14#א': `All forbidden. Mahari Chai wrote: even though inserting his finger was to save wine, nevertheless we are concerned lest after inserting his finger and the wine being saved he will again shake it to pour a libation to avodah zarah — end; and he was not precise in this, for certainly we are not concerned for this at all according to Rashba whom Rama brings in seif 23 — if so there too we should say that initially when he placed his hand on the hole his intent was to save wine and afterward intended to pour a libation like other non-Jews who forbid in preventing wine from coming out; rather certainly all the time there is rescue of wine on his hand there is no concern of libation in it; and likewise inserting his finger in the barrel's hole to save wine; rather he establishes that incident in the Gemara where a non-Jew rested his hand upon it and they forbade it — there was a Jew to save it and the non-Jew preceded and saved it, then he is not called occupied; and this seif is words of Sefer HaTerumah following that Gemara incident mentioned in seif 23 where there was a Jew; but if there was no Jew certainly it is called occupied all the time his finger is inserted for that purpose; and Shulchan Aruch did not mention here anything of this whether there was a Jew, for he follows his reason who holds like the Rosh that saving wine is not called occupation; and Rama did not write here anything since he relied on what he will write in seif 23 to distinguish in this.`,
  '17#_': `And many disagree saying, etc. It appears from his words one should be lenient only without lifting; but when lifting it is called stirring even though he only touches the vessel — namely according to what Darkhei Moshe wrote wanting to establish that Rivevan whom the Tur brings siman 125 does not hold like Rambam here and not as Beit Yosef wrote here that he holds like Rambam — rather Rivevan deals specifically with lifting; but according to what appears to me in my humble opinion one should be lenient even when lifting, for Rashba wrote in Terumat HaDeshen daf 137 that Rambam's words are not clear except we do not find stirring forbids except touching wine itself whether in his body or through something else; but in lifting or carrying and shaking — not, for such is not the way of those who pour libations at all — end; and likewise Raavad, and this is primary — end; and he brings there clear proof for this from a baraita; and such are the Tur's words end of siman 125: a non-Jew carries wine of Israel even if lacking and shakes through his walking — permitted even if open — end; and likewise Mahari Chai like Rashba; and so is primary in my opinion; and all this regarding benefit; but for drinking forbidden through touch alone as I mentioned in siman 123 seif 6 in the name of Raavad; and see what I wrote in siman 125 seif 8 that there are three disputes in the matter; and the language Rama wrote here "and many disagree" are Raavad and Rashba.`,
  '19#א': `Or if he took a barrel and threw it in his anger. Likewise Rambam — this is very astonishing, for in the mishnah it is taught explicitly in this language "this was an incident and they permitted it" — it implies entirely; and likewise Rashi and all poskim explicitly that it is permitted even for drinking, as the Tur and Shulchan Aruch wrote siman 125; and behold in Hagahot Maimoniyot he wrote from this that there is a copying error in Rambam and it must read he threw it not in his anger; and Shulchan Aruch's view who copied Rambam's words as they are — I do not know how to resolve them; also Raavad wrote on these Rambam's words they are corruption — if in his anger permitted even for drinking; and Levush properly did not copy this law of throwing in anger here; and see siman 125 seif 5.`,
  '19#ב': `A non-Jew who treads wine if Israel guarded, etc. In the Tur it is written in this language; and likewise if he treads wine with his feet permitted in benefit since he is occupied treading through something else that Israel guarded he did not touch wine; but if Israel did not guard forbidden in benefit since certainly he touched, for affection of libation is upon him; and Rashba wrote that even if he touched with his feet wine with intention of touch permitted in benefit, for there is no way of libation in that; and impossible — the Rosh forbade it in benefit — end of his words; and in Asheri he did not write this "something else," etc.; and perforce Beit Yosef's view that even if he engaged with his hand in grapes at the time of treading to turn grapes and stop them — permitted in benefit since occupied in his work; and not like the Tur's view who forbids in this by hand; and in my humble opinion the Tur also holds thus, for since treading is considered occupation — what difference foot and what difference hand, all that is the way of treading and its needs, namely to gather grapes; and the Tur did not require guarding except lest he touch not because of his work — for it is possible it is a way of libation by foot even though there is also libation according to the Rosh's view; nevertheless since the foot is always occupied in treading there is no concern in it; but the hand has no need for treading except occasionally to gather grapes — therefore there is concern lest he touch with his hand at a time when there is no occupation in it while he is still treading; and all the more if he bound his hand — for the Rosh holds we do not decree bound against unbound, as the Rosh wrote beginning perek R' Yishmael; and that the Rosh did not write guarding is needed because of hand — there is no need to write it, for since he informs us that even by foot there is prohibition if he does it intentionally — all the more by hand where there is no need, certainly guarding is needed; on this as the law in every place where there is prohibition. And that they challenged the child "behold he pours libation by hand" and needed to say he bound her — for there it comes to teach that even for the latter mishnah there is permission to tread with the non-Jew; and on this they challenged "behold for the latter mishnah he would pour libation from when he began to draw" and needed to answer he bound her hand — namely guarding that we mentioned; nevertheless it is difficult for me why the Tur wrote in siman 123 "and even if he did not touch it but treaded it" one should forbid what is drawn by his force to the pit — behold it is force unintentionally since he is occupied in treading; and end of siman 125 explicit that force of a non-Jew unintentionally is permitted even for drinking — namely he does not know wine comes from the leather bottle; and likewise like it, since he is occupied like in touch there is no distinction between occupied and not knowing it is wine; and it appears to resolve: specifically regarding prohibition that depends on touch — namely that forbids even in benefit because of affection of libation — there is permission if he is occupied in some occupation such as treading or measuring wine even by hand according to the Rosh — then he does not pour libation; but regarding force where there is no way of libation in that but nevertheless the Sages forbade all that comes from his force — if so even if he is occupied in his work nevertheless the essence of his work is to draw wine and this itself the Sages forbade regarding a non-Jew who acts to draw wine from his force; and they did not permit in end of siman 125 force unintentionally except that he does not know he will draw wine at all; and likewise if he does not know it is wine.`,
  '19#ג': `One may not compare matter to matter, etc. It is difficult for me — this is from the Rosh's words who challenged the incident of Rav Papa where the spigot was removed and a non-Jew rested his hand on the hole — why forbidden in benefit, he is occupied saving wine and it is like measuring by hand; and he answered in the name of Raavad: it deals when there were Jews and the non-Jew preceded and placed his hand there — there are others to save and therefore not called occupied; and for libation he did so; and on this the Rosh disagrees: say he did it to find favor; and the original difficulty does not exist, for we have no reason to compare occupations to each other except what we find in the Talmud called occupation — for the agradim who tastes wine was fit to be called occupation like measuring by hand, and nevertheless not called occupied and forbidden in benefit; and likewise saving wine is not called occupation — end. And one may say the Rosh holds the reason there is because he did not stir, as Rashi explained whom Beit Yosef brought nearby; and even though Beit Yosef concludes on Rashi's words — namely it is touch through something else since he intended to save wine — end; in truth Beit Yosef's words here are not in precision, for since Rashi explained he did not stir — permitted in benefit even with intentional touch by his hand; and one may say on Rashi himself: why did he need to explain the reason because of stirring — why not explain it was occupation and touch through something else? One may say he holds like the Rosh that saving wine is not called occupation; and behold Rama ruled in seif 23 like Raavad that saving wine is not called occupation — and how did he write here "you have only what the Sages said," behold we add to them; and what is this addition from another addition. I again saw in Rivash siman 310 in the name of Rashba also like these words that one may not add to the Sages' words; and he himself holds saving wine is occupation; and one may say for Rashba and Raavad who hold that in truth there is this occupation of saving wine in the Gemara — why did they permit in a barrel cracked lengthwise; and not like Rashi who permitted because he did not stir, rather he holds perhaps there was stirring; and likewise in Terumat HaAfelah there was recognizable stirring through the halves.`,
  '21#_': `That he guarded he did not touch, etc. Our rabbi wrote in the name of Rivash siman 310 that this non-Jew is not called occupied in what the Israelite raises, like we find in the Gemara regarding the etrog that fell, as above seif 13 — lest the non-Jew be more panicked to raise his etrog than to raise for Israel, etc.; and if so it was fit to forbid, being touch through something else — namely he touches wine through the Israelite; except he gave a reason (how) [that there is no] applicable touch of a non-Jew in wine — the Israelite touches and not the non-Jew; and likewise we say regarding touch on a vessel that has wine inside — this is not called touch of a non-Jew, rather he touches the vessel and the vessel touches wine, since the vessel already touched wine, etc. — end; and this is like what I wrote in seif 22 that touch through something else requires the toucher to bring the something else and not that it was already there; and this is what Rama wrote "and it is not called touch in what touches through a person" — not specifically person but meaning: since the person was already there it is not called touch — examine carefully; and why did Rivash not permit on grounds that nowadays touch of a non-Jew through something else is permitted for drinking as Rama wrote this siman seif 24.`,
  '22#א': `Behold this is permitted in benefit. But for drinking forbidden, for this is touch of a non-Jew through something else — namely he holds the wine through the two halves of the barrel that were separating were it not for the non-Jew; and one could say: if so let us say regarding touch on a vessel from outside it should be forbidden — touch of wine through the vessel walls; for when the vessel is whole touch does not apply, for all touch through something else means the toucher brings something else at the time of touching to wine; but when the vessel is whole the vessel is already wine inside and there is no touch in wine; but here since the barrel cracked it is as though it separated from wine and separated from it — since it was separating were it not for his holding; therefore properly he brings the something else; and one could say from what the Gemara challenges daf 58 — behold he touched the stopper — learn that this is called touch through something else; for Rashi explained there he thought to draw with a vessel called stopper from the barrel, etc. — found also here the toucher brings the something else to wine; but in benefit permitted because he intends to save wine; and here it deals when there is no Jew to save, as will be explained nearby; and likewise in Beit Yosef in the name of Rashba.`,
  '23#ב': `He is occupied and all is permitted. For drinking it is a copying error and it must read in benefit, for it is no better than measuring in seif 19; and the reasoning that saving wine is called occupation is Raavad's view that I mentioned in seif 19, and Rashba agreed with him; and I saw in Levush who wrote: and specifically in such a case regarding a hole where there is no craft to place hand on the hole and the Jew can also do so; but above regarding a barrel that cracked where there is craft — even if there is a Jew and the non-Jew preceded they did not forbid, etc. — he was not precise at all and the error followed him because he saw Rama arranged this distinction here and not in seif 22; but in truth it refers to everything as Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Rashba to distinguish; and likewise regarding the barrel; and he did not even examine Beit Yosef; and he further wrote the non-Jew did so to find favor, etc. — in this too he was not precise, for this reasoning is neither Raavad's nor the Rosh's as explained in the Rosh I will mention nearby in a responsum. I was asked about a non-Jew who repaired a wine barrel to strengthen it and through striking the barrel a crack opened and wine was spurting through it and he sealed it with his hand; and an Israelite woman stood there with a lamp in her hand to illuminate for him to repair; and meanwhile her husband came and rebuked him and he sealed the crack — what is the law of this wine? Answer: even though at first glance one may say forbidden since there was an Israelite and he preceded and sealed; but after examination it appears permitted, for several times R' Yitzchak in the incident where the spigot was removed from the barrel and a non-Jew jumped and rested his hand upon it — there they forbade in benefit; and the Rosh wrote: why do we forbid in benefit, behold he was occupied saving wine; and Raavad answered there were Jews standing and if not that he intended libation why did he precede — not clear to me, say he did it to find favor, etc.; rather it appears this is not occupation, etc. — end; it implies wherever there is reasoning to be lenient and say he did not intend libation we are lenient; and therefore the Rosh challenged: according to his reasoning that saving wine is considered occupation he should have been lenient even when there are rescuers lest he did it to find favor; and Raavad did not hold this reasoning that for finding favor he nevertheless does not disagree with the Rosh that one may suspend for leniency wherever one can suspend and Raavad did not forbid when there are rescuers except because the non-Jew had no connection to the wine barrel — then there is proof he intended libation, for one who places it for a person who does kindness to save Israel's wine where he can be saved by others — and for this intent itself they said there in the Talmud in Rav Papa's incident that they forbade the wine — interpretation: the non-Jew jumped and sealed, for it is difficult why need to mention this; and even though the incident was so — nevertheless he should have said a non-Jew came and sealed it; and further he said doubled language "a non-Jew came jumped" — since he said came why need jumped; and great proof from what we find in the cracked barrel incident that is permitted — the Gemara said "jumped" and did not say "a non-Jew came jumped" as said regarding Rav Papa's incident which is for prohibition; rather it is clear this is the essence of the proof that the non-Jew intended libation and not because of rescue, for it was not at the barrel's place and uprooted from its place to seal the hole — which is against the reasoning; and likewise proven is the Tur's language here who wrote "and the non-Jew preceded and saved — certainly intended libation" — end; it implies precedence causes; but in the cracked barrel permitted incident it does not deal thus, rather the non-Jew was close to wine at the time of jumping; and all the more in some texts where "jumped" is not mentioned at all in that cracked barrel, etc.; and it was fit to learn from this that if the non-Jew stands at the barrel and the Israelite is somewhat distant — the non-Jew is called occupied; but it does not appear to be so lenient unless distant by measure that there is loss before the Israelite comes; and I again saw thus in the long Terumat HaDeshen daf 135: and here impossible to save quickly through Israel, etc. — end; nevertheless in my humble opinion it is clear if the non-Jew had kosher wine that Israel made and the barrel was pierced and the non-Jew sealed the hole with his hand even if there is a Jew there — he is panicked about his property and therefore hurries more to save his wine; and since there is no proof we suspend to be lenient — end; also in our case where the non-Jew engaged in repairing the barrel and through his actions it was damaged and a hole was made — and by their laws he is liable to pay and also by our laws as found perek HaBayit VeHaAliyah — the builder who demolished a wall and caused damage through his striking is liable to pay — we have no proof he intended libation, rather he was panicked lest he need pay the damage; therefore he preceded and saved and properly we call him occupied; and permitted for us since we establish nowadays touch of non-Jews unintentionally is permitted as Rama wrote in seif 24 — so in my humble opinion; however this matter is novel and was not explicitly mentioned in the poskim, but it is clear and has no doubt in my humble opinion — end.`,
  '23#ה': `In the spurting wine standing in the gutter. Meaning a hollow reed inserted in the barrel's hole and spurting descends through the reed onto wine in the barrel — it is stream capable of stream; but if spurting descends from the barrel itself — what is in the barrel is stream and not capable of stream and forbidden as Beit Yosef wrote end of siman 126 in the name of Rabbeinu Yerucham — namely if there is no great loss as written siman 126; and likewise Beit Yosef explained these words he brought in the name of Kol Bo; except it is difficult for me according to this on what Beit Yosef wrote challenging what the Tur wrote: but if he only touched the spurting that comes out — the wine is permitted; Beit Yosef in one answer said it is stream capable of stream — namely what is below the hole — and if so why was all wine inside, namely even the upper, forbidden — end; the truth is certainly as Beit Yosef wrote in the first answer that then the spurting goes to loss, therefore there is no stream at all; and with this what the Tur wrote and Raavad forbids is resolved, for he holds even though it goes to loss there is stream law for what is above it as the Tur wrote in his name siman 126 regarding Israel pouring to the refuse heap, etc.; and not like Beit Yosef who explained what the Tur wrote in the name of Raavad is from what he wrote to forbid what is below the hole — it is a different matter; rather as I explained in this law itself likewise.`,
  '24#א': `And permitted even for drinking. This is according to Rabbenu Tam in the Tur who disagrees with Rashi — for Rashi there is no distinction between touch of his body and touch through something else — both forbidden in benefit with intention and unintentionally for drinking; but R' Eliezer says touch through something else unintentionally has no name touch at all but the name force; and perforce permitted entirely unintentionally like force permitted in all manners if unintentionally whether he does not know it is wine or does not know of the touch; but Ran wrote a third view of the Geonim's approach who hold like Rabbenu Tam regarding unintentional touch of a non-Jew through something else permitted even for drinking — except they did not give it the name force but it is stricter regarding if he intends touch even though he thinks it is beer — in this forbidden even through something else; and for them Rama's challenge in perek R' Yishmael "behold he touched the stopper" is resolved well — since in any case there is prohibition in this touch even though he did not know it was wine; but for Rabbenu Tam who gives it the name force one must strain and say thus he challenges "behold he touched wine through the stopper" — namely since it is full he touches wine with his body through the stopper; and Beit Yosef challenged whence Ran says the Geonim disagree with Rabbenu Tam on this and it is not difficult at all, for since the Geonim did not give it the name force of a non-Jew — no need to strain like Rabbenu Tam's explanation in what he challenges "behold he touched the stopper"; rather Rabbenu Tam must perforce say thus.`,
  '24#ב': `And likewise if he intended to touch, etc. He stated specifically these two types; but if his intent was touch but he had occupation in one of the occupations mentioned in this siman — he has no permission for drinking; and proof from what they said in the mishnah measuring with a reed and sold; and as the Rosh wrote perek R' Yishmael: and in truth we establish intentional touch without intention is stricter than where there is no intention at all — non-Jew touched a lulav unintentionally permitted for drinking; but if they measured with a reed forbidden for drinking — end of his words; and likewise it appears in Rambam chapter 13 of Forbidden Foods: for touch by hand occupation is worse than no intent of touch, for there in that chapter he wrote a non-Jew touched wine and did not intend for it — behold wine permitted in benefit only; how so — such as fell on a leather bottle of wine or extended hand to a barrel thinking it was oil and found wine — end; and afterward wrote there measuring wine with a reed, etc. — permitted in benefit only — end; it implies but by hand forbidden in benefit; and likewise Beit Yosef according to his view in this siman: by hand occupation does not help to permit in benefit; behold occupation is worse than no intent of touch. And it further appears for Rambam unintentional touch through something else without intent of touch is permitted even for drinking like Rabbenu Tam; and Rif — if like Rashi who forbids for drinking why write he extended hand to barrel and thinks it was oil — not permitted except in benefit to write a stringency even touch through something else; rather certainly through something else truly permitted even for drinking since unintentional touch or he does not know it is wine; but regarding occupation he holds through his hand forbidden even in benefit and through something else permitted in benefit — and this is clear; and I am astonished Beit Yosef did not notice this to bring Rambam's view on this that he also holds permitted for drinking; and I have a place to be astonished here: since Rabbenu Tam's reason for touch through something else permitted for drinking is because touch through something else is not called touch but only the name force — as Ran brought whom Beit Yosef brought — in this there is a distinction between Geonim and Rabbenu Tam: for Geonim did not give it the name force and perforce forbidden with intent of touch even though he did not know it was wine, and permission is only without intent of touch at all; but for Rabbenu Tam who gives it the name force — permitted even with intent of touch except he does not know it is wine; and likewise the Tur to permit also in this; perforce the Tur holds here there is the name force in touch through something else; and it is astonishing accordingly: with touch through something else with intention there is prohibition only for drinking like force with intention; and likewise Ran explicitly whom Beit Yosef brought: and I already wrote Rabbenu Tam holds touch through something else is only his force; and according to one who says his force even with intention does not forbid except for drinking — likewise the law in his touch through something else — end; and if so why did the Tur write in siman 125 in conclusion there is no prohibition in force with intention except for drinking, and in siman 124 wrote touch through something else with intention forbids even in benefit — behold both have one force; and it is very difficult to resolve.`,
  '24#ג': `All their touch is called unintentional. Meaning they have no intent to pour libation and it is like a child in this siman who forbids only for drinking; and wherever there is one favorable aspect — permitted for drinking for us; except Rama mentioned only two matters: if he touched with his hand — namely without intent of touch; or he did not know it was wine — it implies he intended touch and knew it was wine but has occupation — not permitted, for I already brought proof nearby from the Rosh's words that occupation does not permit like those two; and likewise explained in responsum Rav Laviev siman 41 from which is the source of this Rama's law: in siman 41 regarding an incident where they made wine from raisins and filled the barrel with water and on Shabbat it was very fermenting and spilled through boiling and they called a non-Jew to shake the barrel with one staff so it would not spill — he answered on this that this is complete wine since it had three days, etc.; rather one may permit on grounds Rashbam wrote in the name of Rashi that gentiles in our time are not idolaters; and combine with this what Rambam wrote: wherever we said in this matter regarding our wine that it is forbidden for drinking and permitted in benefit because of a side of touch that a non-Jew idolater touched it; but if its prohibition is because of a non-Jew who is not an idolater, such as an Ishmaelite who touched our wine unintentionally or patted the mouth of the barrel — behold permitted for drinking; and likewise all similar cases — end; behold if we take these two reasons it is possible in the case before us to permit the wine for drinking if the non-Jews' touch was only through something else — then even if she knows the nature of avodah zarah, since her touch is without intent of libation it does not forbid except for drinking; and since we hold her as not knowing we descend a level and permit even for drinking; but if she touched with her hand one may not permit in any manner for drinking, for if we held her as knowing the nature of avodah zarah we would forbid even in benefit; and now we descend a level and permit in benefit but not for drinking — end. What the rabbi wrote: if we held her as knowing the nature of avodah zarah it would be forbidden even in benefit — this is according to Rambam who holds touch by his hand even if he has occupation forbidden in benefit, as Shulchan Aruch wrote seif 19; and perforce Mahari Chai wrote: for us who establish like the Rosh whom he brings there seif 19 that he is not forbidden in benefit in occupation even in touch by hand — perforce when we descend a level it will be regarding idolaters in our time permitted even for drinking if he has occupation such as saving wine even touching with his hand in wine to save it; and if so we grasp for us the leniency of Rambam regarding descending a level from idolaters to non-Jew in our time and the leniency of the Rosh for Talmudic law of touch by hand; and perforce for us permitted for drinking; but for Rambam certainly forbidden for us for drinking in touch by hand with occupation; and perforce Rambam wrote the permission in Ishmaelite if he touched unintentionally — namely without intent of touch at all — that by Talmudic law permitted in benefit alone in touch by hand as Rambam explicitly wrote in chapter 12 of Forbidden Foods — therefore in this specifically permitted in Ishmaelite; and likewise patting the mouth of the barrel — in this too by Talmudic law permitted in benefit since there is no way of libation in that even though he touches by hand; therefore we need to combine the Rosh's view we mentioned; and examine carefully that the leniency we mentioned in the name of Rambam of descending a level for non-Jew in our time helps only for unintentional touch or he does not know it is wine to permit for drinking; but occupation does not help in this even according to the Rosh, for the Rosh wrote beginning perek R' Yishmael: and it is possible those who practiced permission of benefit in touch of a non-Jew relied on this — we establish like the rabbis of R' Nasan who forbid measuring by hand — sell; idolaters in our time all their touch is like touch of measuring; and further all their touch cannot come to libation — end; behold this reasoning does not help for permission of benefit; and one may say there are two favorable aspects here — one from the non-Jew himself being like measuring; and further he is truly occupied in measuring and similar in saving wine — this is not so, for the essence of the leniency in occupation of measuring or similar is because there is no intent of libation — what difference if he has this advantage once namely from himself not fit for libation and what difference if he has another time from being occupied in measuring and not intending libation; and further if so regarding an idolatrous child we should say by Talmudic law his touch permitted even for drinking since he has two favorable aspects — one from himself not expert in avodah zarah and second from not intending touch as the Rosh wrote there that Rav's reason the child does not make yayin nesech to forbid in benefit is because he does not intend touch and not because he does not know avodah zarah — for if so it should be permitted even for drinking like children of maidservants; and if so there is a difficulty what is truly the reason — occupation does not help as you would say regarding non-Jew in our time with two favorable aspects why combination helps to permit drinking and all the more the child in Talmudic times should help; rather certainly combination does not help; and one may say behold we find combination of two favorable aspects helps — namely unintentional touch through something else permitted even for drinking even by Talmudic law; this is not because combination helps but because touch of a non-Jew through something else is called his force alone for Rabbenu Tam whom we establish like; and in his force permitted unintentionally — therefore also in touch through something else permitted unintentionally. Further it is difficult for me on permission of drinking for us in touch of non-Jews by hand with occupation because of Rambam's words we mentioned — if so we descend a level; if so there is difficulty on those poskim who hold touch through something else with intention forbids only for drinking as Beit Yosef wrote in this siman in the name of Ran that Rabbenu Tam holds thus — why do we say in the mishnah non-Jews measured with a reed — sell; why not say we descend a level and through occupation of measuring it should be permitted for drinking since even with intention there is no prohibition of benefit; what difference non-Jews in our time from touch of non-Jews through something else of Talmudic times — in both there is only prohibition of drinking; and why would combination of occupation help more here than there; rather perforce one must say combination of occupation does not help to permit drinking at all, except specifically without intent of touch; and as Rama wrote here; and this did not enter that level Rambam mentioned that Ishmaelite has over other non-Jews — specifically in what he mentioned namely without intent of touch, etc., he has an advantage. Also from the Tur there is proof for prohibition, for he wrote unintentional touch of a non-Jew through something else — such as he has a reed in his hand and touched wine unintentionally or with intent of touch but does not know it is wine, etc. — permitted for drinking for practical halachah; behold he mentioned only these two but did not permit because of occupation — rather combination of occupation does not help at all for permission of drinking; and likewise regarding non-Jew for us it does not help; nevertheless since the elder Mahari Chai already ruled to be lenient for drinking in saving wine by placing his hand on the hole — one may give a reason: since there is no touch in all the wine but only preventing it from coming out, there is only prohibition of force of non-Jews as Ran wrote in perek R' Yishmael in the name of Ramban in Rav Papa's incident where the spigot was removed — all wine that was fit to come out is called his force and forbidden for drinking; and force unintentionally the rabbis did not decree on it; and likewise Rambam chapter 12 of Forbidden Foods in this language; and found everything comes from his force — one may say also in this matter that since he has occupation in saving wine he is called for this purpose unintentional and permitted for drinking; but if he placed a finger in the hole passing over the rims, even though he does so to save, since there is actual touch inside as all poskim and Tosafot wrote in the name of Sefer HaTerumah — forbidden even for us for drinking as stated.`,
  '24#ד': `And likewise if he touched even with his hand, etc. In Maharil's responsum siman 38 he wrote that a wine barrel was in a non-Jew's hand sealed well with the stopper, only in the stopper there was concern lest he drank through a hollow reed; and he ruled permitted even if he drank through that reed, being touch of a non-Jew through something else; and if because of a returning drop of wine nullified in sixty; and he wrote there is no concern here except lest he drew through a bent spigot that Shulchan Aruch mentioned in seif 23 — this is not common and not everyone knows that craft — end; and accordingly in Galilee where bent spigot is common one should certainly forbid; nevertheless we learn here there is no prohibition if he drinks through a hollow reed; and in truth this matter is discussed in dispute, for it is found in the Gemara perek R' Yishmael (Avodah Zarah daf 58): a non-Jew agradim — meaning appointed by the king over measures — pierced with a spigot; Rashi: hollow reed and raised wine to his mouth; and we say there forbidden in benefit all who know it is wine; and the Rosh wrote there it appears from Tosafot the drop of wine that was in his mouth forbids everything in any amount, etc. — it implies forbidden only from mixture; perforce for us who establish in siman 134 stam yeinam nullified in sixty — permitted in the case above; and likewise it appears from Rambam chapter 12 of Forbidden Foods that prohibition is from mixture from what was in his mouth and fell there; but Ran wrote there wine is forbidden entirely because the lower became a base for the upper and it is like touching all wine in the barrel; and from the Tur's words who considered this spigot law under prohibition of non-Jew's touch — it appears he also holds like Ran, for he compared this to drinking from the barrel; and Rashba in Terumat HaDeshen daf 133 wrote like the Rosh; and likewise Tosafot in perek Gid HaNasheh (daf 97 end of side 1) regarding the spigot case: if the non-Jew preceded and ruled forbidden — meaning l'chatchila but b'di'eved permitted, for prohibition is only because it fell from his mouth and it is nullified in sixty; and since Ran and the Tur are alone against them — we establish to permit as Maharil wrote above. And in Derishah siman 130 he wrote: one must give a reason why we do not permit b'di'eved in our time if a non-Jew drank through a tube in a small hole in a barrel, etc.; and he brought Ran's words and it was already explained one should not rule like Ran here; but the language he wrote "why we do not permit," etc. — it implies it is customary practice to forbid and we did not hear such custom to forbid; on the contrary I recall in my youth such a case occurred in Pozna and all scholars there agreed to permit, and so is the essence; and one should not be stringent in this in a place of great loss — so in my humble opinion. And likewise if the non-Jew drew wine by hand from the barrel through a small spigot that wine merchants regularly use, and drew an amount the spigot holds and he touches with his hand wine at the mouth of the spigot — likewise one may permit b'di'eved in great loss if returned to the barrel, for it is one law. And what he wrote in the hagahah permitted and one should not publicize, etc. — in my humble opinion even though Rama wrote to be lenient regarding touch of non-Jews in our time — nevertheless force of non-Jews forbids even in our time, for Rambam wrote in this law simply in chapter 12 of Forbidden Foods and beginning chapter 1 wrote a general rule: wherever stam prohibition is written — regarding idolater for prohibition of benefit, and regarding non-idolater such as Ishmaelite for prohibition of drinking; perforce also regarding non-Jew in our time it is so; nevertheless in my humble opinion one should be lenient in this in all manners, for prohibition is only in the lower and not the upper — in such stream it is obvious we do not say connection.`,
  '24#ה': `And they pierced there with a knife, etc. In Mordechai it is written: perhaps he did not touch wine but immediately when the knife passed the thickness of the rims wine came out and he did not touch it in wine, etc.; and further even if the knife touched it is permitted since the thief did not intend except that wine would come out through piercing with a knife where it is possible he would not touch wine — he permitted; but if the spigot was inserted passing all the rims and the non-Jew drew out the spigot forbidden even for us — being touch with intent in wine through the spigot; and it is difficult — behold Mordechai already wrote and Rama ruled nearby that for us touch of a non-Jew through something else is permitted even for drinking; and likewise difficulty on what Rama wrote end of siman 129 regarding a maidservant not believed to say she took wine from the barrel — it implies if she clearly took it, even wine in the barrel forbidden; and likewise it appears siman 129 seif 1; and it appears the reason for prohibition even for us when the non-Jew drew through a spigot — it is close he touched with his hand the spurting that comes out; and Beit Yosef already wrote end of siman 126 in the name of Rabbeinu Yerucham that if he touched the spurting all is forbidden in benefit as though he touched all the wine — namely from stream connection (and even though we) permit stream at beginning of siman 126 — nevertheless one should be concerned lest he touched wine at the place where it actually comes out, for such is the custom of one engaged in drawing wine from a barrel through a spigot, and it is actual touch; and if the spigot is inserted in a hollow reed where one cannot say so — one may permit wine in the barrel in great loss as written beginning siman 126 in the law of stream connection; and likewise I saw in Mordechai perek R' Yishmael in the law of one who asked about the non-Jewess who entered the cellar, etc. — when she drew the spigot and returned it impossible she did not touch with her hand the spurting that comes out, etc. — end; and in perek HaSocher he further distinguishes between narrow and wide hole — easily one touches in wide — end; therefore Rama also did not permit here except piercing with a knife which is a small hole and not close to touch in drawing the knife in thin spurting that comes out — so it appears to me; and see what I wrote in siman 130 seif 1 that here it deals when the knife is still there, but if the non-Jew already sealed it, forbidden.`,
  '27#א': `One must be careful, etc. In the Tur he concluded with this: and if he does not touch with his hand but with a knife it is permitted since he intends only to seal the vessel and not to touch wine — being like unintentional touch through something else; and Beit Yosef challenged: behold here there is in any case intent of touch; and it is difficult, for according to Rabbenu Tam whom the Tur holds like — unintentional touch through something else permitted even for drinking — namely even if he intended touch but does not know it is wine permitted; likewise here where he intends to seal the vessel it is like not knowing there is wine there, for he has no business with wine; and do not challenge from the mishnah that said measuring with a reed — sell, for there in any case his main business is with wine. And it appears to explain the Tur's words in what he first wrote "an Israelite should take the oakum," etc.; and Beit Yosef challenged: if so this is the latter part of drinking much wine; and further why should the non-Jew not give the oakum; and it appears: even though we find unintentional touch through something else permitted for drinking — namely b'di'eved; but l'chatchila it is not fit to do this way — therefore the Tur comes to inform us a stringency in the first part and in the latter part: in the first part where there is not much wine — it informs us even so l'chatchila the Israelite should give oakum; except if there is much wine there is stringency that extra guarding is needed lest he touch wine — unlike in the first part where extra warning is not needed on this; but nevertheless also in the latter part if he has extra guarding that he does not touch wine — permitted even l'chatchila if the non-Jew does not give oakum but Israel does as in the first part; but b'di'eved permitted even in the latter part if the non-Jew gives oakum — being unintentional touch through something else; except it is still difficult for me what he wrote regarding the mishnah sprinkling the hornet with a reed forbidden for drinking and not called his touch through something else — behold there he has no need for wine at all except to sprinkle the hornet, and nevertheless since impossible except that he will touch wine forbidden; likewise here sealing the hole with oakum of flax — since impossible except he will touch wine with the knife — for the flax is soaked in wine as it seals the spurting; and one may say flax is not called wine in what is absorbed inside, for if so why need a knife — let it suffice being something else through the flax; rather certainly flax is like wine since it is absorbed and soiled in wine; and Beit Yosef resolved the challenge from what is written here from measuring with a reed — sell — with two answers, namely in the first answer here one may be lenient since he only touches what drips — and it is difficult what difference much wine little wine; and as he himself wrote in siman 126 in "there are those who say" regarding stream, etc. — what difference pouring into much wine, etc.; and the second answer is obvious it does not help this difficulty from hornet, for also regarding hornet he intends to drive wine from the hornet and is very similar to sealing the hole to drive spurting water from there; and one must distinguish: hornet is different, for nevertheless he touches clear wine in a place where libation applies — unlike here where he touches knife to oakum even though absorbed in wine nevertheless libation does not apply there at all. And further one may say since what goes outside goes to loss there is no concern in what goes out as Mordechai wrote perek R' Yishmael — perforce also what is absorbed in flax is like spurting going to loss; nevertheless l'chatchila there is prohibition to leave the non-Jew at the spurting — therefore he must tighten with knife specifically.`,
};

export const FILES = [
  ['siman_124/turei-zahav/part-001.txt', 'turei-zahav'],
  ['siman_124/turei-zahav/part-002.txt', 'turei-zahav'],
];
